## How we work: specification-driven development

No code gets written until there is an approved spec for it.

```
idea  ──/idea──▶ docs/ideas/NNNN-slug.md                 (original wording + Q&A log; always at the root)
      ──/spec──▶ <level>/docs/specs/NNNN-slug/spec.md    (WHAT and WHY; level = root or apps/<app>, by scope)
      ──/plan──▶ plan.md + tasks.md                      (HOW: design; ADRs at the right level; architecture docs updated)
      ──/implement──▶ code + tests                       (test-first; quality gate passes)
      ──/review-spec──▶ independent check + metrics snapshot
```

- `NNNN` is shared: idea `0003` becomes spec `0003-*`, wherever it lives. Root ADRs and each app's ADRs have their own numbering.
- **Scope** of an idea is `system` (several apps, libraries, shared data, repo tooling) or one app. `/spec` decides it with the user and records it in the idea and the spec.
- Run each stage in a **fresh session** (`/clear`), so the files carry the context instead of the chat history.
- Other skills: `/scaffold` creates an app, library or dataset; `/adr` records a decision; `/arch-metrics` computes and stores metrics; `/add-language` sets up the quality gate for a new programming language.

## Rules

1. **Never rewrite an original idea.** The `## Original idea` section is the user's exact words.
2. **Log every question.** Each question you ask the user (in chat or through AskUserQuestion), the options you offered and the user's answer go into the related idea's `## Q&A log`, right after the answer arrives. Questions the user asks about an idea, and your answers, are logged there too.
3. **The spec is the source of truth.** If you find mid-work that it is wrong, stop, ask (and log it), update the spec, then continue.
4. **Every requirement needs a check.** Each acceptance criterion must be testable, and the plan names the test that verifies it.
5. **Architecture docs change in the same change as the code, at the right level.** A new or re-wired app, library or dataset changes the root `components.json` (then run `tools/monorepo/system-map.sh`). A component inside an app changes that app's `components.json` and `architecture.md`. Decisions another app could notice (libraries, a dataset contract, a base env variable, repo tooling, a rule for all apps) are **root ADRs**; decisions inside one app are **app ADRs** (`/adr --app <app>`). Accepted ADRs are never edited, only superseded.
6. **Apps are loosely coupled.** An app never imports from another app. Apps share code only through libraries: the public entry points of `libs/<lib>` listed in their `mayDependOn`, and share data only through datasets: one owner writes, readers rely on the contract in `libs/contracts/<dataset>/` (ADR-0013). A breaking contract change needs a root ADR and a change for every reader. Libraries may use other libraries the same way (no cycles), never an app (ADR-0015).
7. **High cohesion, low coupling are enforced, not hoped for**, at both levels. No dependency cycles. Functions stay within the complexity limits in [docs/architecture/metrics/README.md](docs/architecture/metrics/README.md). Don't weaken a threshold or a boundary rule without an ADR.
8. **Configuration comes from the layered environment** (ADR-0012): process env > `apps/<app>/.env` > root `.env`. Run apps through `tools/env/with-env.sh <app> -- <command>`. A new variable goes into the matching `.env.example` (root if every app needs it, else the app's) in the same change. Never read, print or ask for the contents of a `.env` file; use `with-env.sh <app> --print` to see which keys are set.
9. **New programming language ⇒ `/add-language` first.** A hook blocks writing source files in a language that is not registered in [tools/quality/languages.json](tools/quality/languages.json). Do not get around it (for example by writing the file through Bash).
10. **{% if visibility == 'public' %}This repo is public.{% else %}Treat this repo as public: private repos get published later.{% endif %}** Never commit secrets, tokens, personal data or shared runtime data. Never bypass the hooks (`--no-verify`). If gitleaks flags something, remove it; only allowlist a confirmed false positive in `.gitleaks.toml`, with a comment.
11. **Keep status fields and indexes current**: the `Status:` lines, [docs/ideas/README.md](docs/ideas/README.md), [docs/specs/README.md](docs/specs/README.md) (all specs, both levels), and the ADR index at the level where an ADR was added.

## Conventions

- After cloning: `brew install gitleaks pre-commit jq && pre-commit install`, then `cp .env.example .env` and fill it in. Check an app's environment: `tools/env/with-env.sh <app> --check`. Run every check: `pre-commit run --all-files`.
- GitHub protection (once, after the first push): `tools/bootstrap-github.sh`.
- This project was generated from [sdd-template-for-monorepo-with-claude](https://github.com/belaboros/sdd-template-for-monorepo-with-claude). Pull template improvements with `copier update --trust` on a clean branch, and review the diff. Never edit `.copier-answers.yml` by hand.
- Names of apps, libraries and datasets are kebab-case. Dates are absolute (`YYYY-MM-DD`). Diagrams are Mermaid in Markdown (ADR-0004).
- Tech stack, build, test, lint and metrics commands: _added here (repo-wide) or in `apps/<app>/CLAUDE.md` (per app) by `/add-language` and `/plan`._
